WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.433
Data and pipeline testing.

2
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:07.224
Many AI failure stories start with the words, the model was fine, but.

3
00:00:07.224 --> 00:00:08.700
They end with bad data.

4
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:10.973
The rule is simple.

5
00:00:10.973 --> 00:00:13.233
Test the data before you test the model.

6
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:21.933
A null heavy column, a units change nobody flagged, or a parser that quietly returns empty text can ruin everything downstream.

7
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:25.533
Here is the idea of a data contract.

8
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:28.500
Raw data comes in from your ingestion job.

9
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.267
A schema checks every batch, the same discipline as an API contract.

10
00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:35.956
Only batches that pass reach the index.

11
00:00:35.956 --> 00:00:37.733
Everything else fails loudly.

12
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:41.700
A contract usually combines four kinds of check.

13
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.773
Schema validation checks types, required columns, and allowed ranges.

14
00:00:46.773 --> 00:00:50.300
Pandera, Great Expectations, or Pydantic can do it.

15
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:56.767
Statistical checks compare today's distribution with a reference window, instead of eyeballing a dashboard.

16
00:00:57.067 --> 00:01:00.467
Completeness checks watch null rates and empty parsed text.

17
00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:06.087
Referential checks make sure every chunk points to a source document that still exists.

18
00:01:06.087 --> 00:01:09.633
Silent orphaning looks like a retrieval problem for weeks.

19
00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:12.600
Here is a contract in Pandera.

20
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.081
Each column gets a type and a rule.

21
00:01:15.081 --> 00:01:20.067
The text must be longer than twenty characters, and the page cannot be negative.

22
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:23.061
Then the ingest function validates first.

23
00:01:23.061 --> 00:01:27.333
If the batch is bad, it raises before anything touches the index.

24
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:31.067
Now a real story from Mission Control RAG.

25
00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:34.065
The team swaps in a faster PDF parser.

26
00:01:34.065 --> 00:01:36.833
It ingests cleanly, with no exceptions.

27
00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:40.433
But every scanned appendix now comes back as empty text.

28
00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:44.121
A text length check in the contract catches it the same day.

29
00:01:44.121 --> 00:01:48.300
Without it, you hear two weeks later that the bot doesn't know appendix C.

30
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.215
Let's recap.

31
00:01:50.215 --> 00:01:52.600
Test the data before you test the model.

32
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:56.500
A data contract fails loudly at the ingestion boundary.

33
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.967
Check schema, statistics, completeness, and references.

34
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:03.733
That way silent regressions get caught the same day.

35
00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:07.000
Next up: model and RAG evals as regression tests.
